   "name": "l1_inclass",
   "comment": "DISCOVERS whether the lighting change between this frame and its reference is in the GLOBAL DIAGONAL class, now at REAL-VALUED gains. T3's li_inclass demands EXACT integer cross-multiplication, which a rounded real gain breaks -- that is precisely why T3 measured 0/12 at gains 1.5/2.5/3.3/7.5. This rule replaces the exact equality with the ROUNDING ENVELOPE THE LATTICE ITSELF IMPLIES: the displacement of the order-statistic identity is a sum of half-lattice-step roundings, each scaled by its own cross-multiplier, and nothing more. TWO clauses must hold on ALL THREE channels -- the SUM clause (T3's own statistic, envelope 1/2*(ref_s + n*ref_a), loose because the sum accumulates n roundings) and the tighter MIN clause (envelope 1/2*(ref_m + ref_a), only two roundings). NO TOLERANCE IS TUNED: every 1/2 is the maximum displacement of a single round(), and n is COUNTED from the data. Holds => mint an IlluminantL1 carrying the REAL per-channel gain Div(l1_a, ref.l1_a). A REAL number is CORRECT here and is not a violation of the frame/continuum invariant: the gain is a CROSS-FRAME MAP, never an in-frame coordinate -- the surfaces' cones stay integers. This is exactly how continuous pose holds a real theta inside Rotate. Fails => nothing minted, l1_inclass stays 0, l1_recognize cannot fire: a spatially-varying (multi-illuminant) change is REFUSED, not force-normalised. Flag-gated, OFF by default (sec 9).",
